@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.785 = private unnamed_addr constant [798 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the `yaml.load` function.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ARunning the `yaml.load` function over untrusted YAML files is insecure, as\0A`yaml.load` allows for the creation of arbitrary Python objects, which can\0Athen be used to execute arbitrary code.\0A\0AInstead, consider using `yaml.safe_load`, which allows for the creation of\0Asimple Python objects like integers and lists, but prohibits the creation of\0Amore complex objects like functions and classes.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport yaml\0A\0Ayaml.load(untrusted_yaml)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport yaml\0A\0Ayaml.safe_load(untrusted_yaml)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [PyYAML documentation: Loading YAML](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-20](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\0A", align 1
