@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.793 = private unnamed_addr constant [731 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of cryptographic keys with vulnerable key sizes.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ASmall keys are easily breakable. For DSA and RSA, keys should be at least\0A2048 bits long. For EC, keys should be at least 224 bits long.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import dsa, ec\0A\0Adsa.generate_private_key(key_size=512)\0Aec.generate_private_key(curve=ec.SECT163K1())\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives.asymmetric import dsa, ec\0A\0Adsa.generate_private_key(key_size=4096)\0Aec.generate_private_key(curve=ec.SECP384R1())\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [CSRC: Transitioning the Use of Cryptographic Algorithms and Key Lengths](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/131/a/r2/final)\0A", align 1
