@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.840 = private unnamed_addr constant [558 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for dictionary comprehensions that use a static key, like a string\0Aliteral or a variable defined outside the comprehension.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUsing a static key (like a string literal) in a dictionary comprehension\0Ais usually a mistake, as it will result in a dictionary with only one key,\0Adespite the comprehension iterating over multiple values.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adata = [\22some\22, \22Data\22]\0A{\22key\22: value.upper() for value in data}\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adata = [\22some\22, \22Data\22]\0A{value: value.upper() for value in data}\0A```\0A", align 1
