@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.855 = private unnamed_addr constant [765 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `contextlib.suppress` without arguments.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`contextlib.suppress` is a context manager that suppresses exceptions. It takes,\0Aas arguments, the exceptions to suppress within the enclosed block. If no\0Aexceptions are specified, then the context manager won't suppress any\0Aexceptions, and is thus redundant.\0A\0AConsider adding exceptions to the `contextlib.suppress` call, or removing the\0Acontext manager entirely.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport contextlib\0A\0Awith contextlib.suppress():\0A    foo()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport contextlib\0A\0Awith contextlib.suppress(Exception):\0A    foo()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `contextlib.suppress`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/contextlib.html#contextlib.suppress)\0A", align 1
