@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.956 = private unnamed_addr constant [863 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the presence of multiple literal types in a union.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`Literal[\22foo\22, 42]` has identical semantics to\0A`Literal[\22foo\22] | Literal[42]`, but is clearer and more concise.\0A\0A## Example\0A```pyi\0Afrom typing import Literal\0A\0Afield: Literal[1] | Literal[2] | str\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```pyi\0Afrom typing import Literal\0A\0Afield: Literal[1, 2] | str\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis fix is marked unsafe if it would delete any comments within the replacement range.\0A\0AAn example to illustrate where comments are preserved and where they are not:\0A\0A```pyi\0Afrom typing import Literal\0A\0Afield: (\0A    # deleted comment\0A    Literal[\22a\22, \22b\22]  # deleted comment\0A    # deleted comment\0A    | Literal[\22c\22, \22d\22]  # preserved comment\0A)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `typing.Literal`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.Literal)\0A", align 1
