@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1087 = private unnamed_addr constant [1191 x i8] c"## Removed\0AThis rule is identical to [SIM101] which should be used instead.\0A\0A## What it does\0AChecks for repeated `isinstance` calls on the same object.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ARepeated `isinstance` calls on the same object can be merged into a\0Asingle call.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe on Python 3.10 and later, as combining\0Amultiple `isinstance` calls with a binary operator (`|`) will fail at\0Aruntime if any of the operands are themselves tuples.\0A\0AFor example, given `TYPES = (dict, list)`, then\0A`isinstance(None, TYPES | set | float)` will raise a `TypeError` at runtime,\0Awhile `isinstance(None, set | float)` will not.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef is_number(x):\0A    return isinstance(x, int) or isinstance(x, float) or isinstance(x, complex)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef is_number(x):\0A    return isinstance(x, (int, float, complex))\0A```\0A\0AOr, for Python 3.10 and later:\0A\0A```python\0Adef is_number(x):\0A    return isinstance(x, int | float | complex)\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `isinstance`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#isinstance)\0A\0A[SIM101]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/duplicate-isinstance-call/\0A", align 1
