@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1098 = private unnamed_addr constant [1037 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `subprocess.run` without an explicit `check` argument.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABy default, `subprocess.run` does not check the return code of the process\0Ait runs. This can lead to silent failures.\0A\0AInstead, consider using `check=True` to raise an exception if the process\0Afails, or set `check=False` explicitly to mark the behavior as intentional.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport subprocess\0A\0Asubprocess.run([\22ls\22, \22nonexistent\22])  # No exception raised.\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport subprocess\0A\0Asubprocess.run([\22ls\22, \22nonexistent\22], check=True)  # Raises exception.\0A```\0A\0AOr:\0A```python\0Aimport subprocess\0A\0Asubprocess.run([\22ls\22, \22nonexistent\22], check=False)  # Explicitly no check.\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe for function calls that contain\0A`**kwargs`, as adding a `check` keyword argument to such a call may lead\0Ato a duplicate keyword argument error.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `subprocess.run`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html#subprocess.run)\0A", align 1
