@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1102 = private unnamed_addr constant [1060 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for type names that do not match the variance of their associated\0Atype parameter.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 484] recommends the use of the `_co` and `_contra` suffixes for\0Acovariant and contravariant type parameters, respectively (while invariant\0Atype parameters should not have any such suffix).\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import TypeVar\0A\0AT = TypeVar(\22T\22, covariant=True)\0AU = TypeVar(\22U\22, contravariant=True)\0AV_co = TypeVar(\22V_co\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom typing import TypeVar\0A\0AT_co = TypeVar(\22T_co\22, covariant=True)\0AU_contra = TypeVar(\22U_contra\22, contravariant=True)\0AV = TypeVar(\22V\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `typing` \E2\80\94 Support for type hints](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html)\0A- [PEP 483 \E2\80\93 The Theory of Type Hints: Covariance and Contravariance](https://peps.python.org/pep-0483/#covariance-and-contravariance)\0A- [PEP 484 \E2\80\93 Type Hints: Covariance and contravariance](https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/#covariance-and-contravariance)\0A\0A[PEP 484]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0484/\0A", align 1
