@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1129 = private unnamed_addr constant [1592 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary default type arguments for `Generator` and\0A`AsyncGenerator` on Python 3.13+.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APython 3.13 introduced the ability for type parameters to specify default\0Avalues. Following this change, several standard-library classes were\0Aupdated to add default values for some of their type parameters. For\0Aexample, `Generator[int]` is now equivalent to\0A`Generator[int, None, None]`, as the second and third type parameters of\0A`Generator` now default to `None`.\0A\0AOmitting type arguments that match the default values can make the code\0Amore concise and easier to read.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Afrom collections.abc import Generator, AsyncGenerator\0A\0A\0Adef sync_gen() -> Generator[int, None, None]:\0A    yield 42\0A\0A\0Aasync def async_gen() -> AsyncGenerator[int, None]:\0A    yield 42\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom collections.abc import Generator, AsyncGenerator\0A\0A\0Adef sync_gen() -> Generator[int]:\0A    yield 42\0A\0A\0Aasync def async_gen() -> AsyncGenerator[int]:\0A    yield 42\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as safe, unless the type annotation contains comments.\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 696 \E2\80\93 Type Defaults for Type Parameters](https://peps.python.org/pep-0696/)\0A- [Annotating generators and coroutines](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#annotating-generators-and-coroutines)\0A- [Python documentation: `typing.Generator`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.Generator)\0A- [Python documentation: `typing.AsyncGenerator`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.AsyncGenerator)\0A", align 1
