@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1147 = private unnamed_addr constant [1200 x i8] c"## Deprecation\0AThis rule was deprecated as using [PEP 604] syntax in `isinstance` and `issubclass` calls\0Aisn't recommended practice, and it incorrectly suggests that other typing syntaxes like [PEP 695]\0Awould be supported by `isinstance` and `issubclass`. Using the [PEP 604] syntax\0Ais also slightly slower.\0A\0A## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `isinstance` and `issubclass` that take a tuple\0Aof types for comparison.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ASince Python 3.10, `isinstance` and `issubclass` can be passed a\0A`|`-separated union of types, which is consistent\0Awith the union operator introduced in [PEP 604].\0A\0ANote that this results in slower code. Ignore this rule if the\0Aperformance of an `isinstance` or `issubclass` check is a\0Aconcern, e.g., in a hot loop.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aisinstance(x, (int, float))\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aisinstance(x, int | float)\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `isinstance`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#isinstance)\0A- [Python documentation: `issubclass`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#issubclass)\0A\0A[PEP 604]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0604/\0A[PEP 695]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0695/\0A", align 1
