@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1212 = private unnamed_addr constant [914 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for consistent style regarding whether nonempty tuples in subscripts\0Aare parenthesized.\0A\0AThe exact nature of this violation depends on the setting\0A[`lint.ruff.parenthesize-tuple-in-subscript`]. By default, the use of\0Aparentheses is considered a violation.\0A\0AThis rule is not applied inside \22typing contexts\22 (type annotations,\0Atype aliases and subscripted class bases), as these have their own specific\0Aconventions around them.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIt is good to be consistent and, depending on the codebase, one or the other\0Aconvention may be preferred.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Adirections = {(0, 1): \22North\22, (1, 0): \22East\22, (0, -1): \22South\22, (-1, 0): \22West\22}\0Adirections[(0, 1)]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead (with default setting):\0A\0A```python\0Adirections = {(0, 1): \22North\22, (1, 0): \22East\22, (0, -1): \22South\22, (-1, 0): \22West\22}\0Adirections[0, 1]\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.ruff.parenthesize-tuple-in-subscript`\0A", align 1
