@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1216 = private unnamed_addr constant [23 x i8] c"missing-f-string-syntax", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1217 = private unnamed_addr constant [1947 x i8] c"## What it does\0ASearches for strings that look like they were meant to be f-strings, but are missing an `f` prefix.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AExpressions inside curly braces are only evaluated if the string has an `f` prefix.\0A\0A## Details\0A\0AThere are many possible string literals which are not meant to be f-strings\0Adespite containing f-string-like syntax. As such, this lint ignores all strings\0Awhere one of the following conditions applies:\0A\0A1. The string is a standalone expression. For example, the rule ignores all docstrings.\0A2. The string is part of a function call with argument names that match at least one variable\0A   (for example: `format(\22Message: {value}\22, value=\22Hello World\22)`)\0A3. The string (or a parent expression of the string) has a direct method call on it\0A   (for example: `\22{value}\22.format(...)`)\0A4. The string has no `{...}` expression sections, or uses invalid f-string syntax.\0A5. The string references variables that are not in scope, or it doesn't capture variables at all.\0A6. Any format specifiers in the potential f-string are invalid.\0A7. The string is part of a function call that is known to expect a template string rather than an\0A   evaluated f-string: for example, a [`logging`][logging] call, a [`gettext`][gettext] call,\0A   or a [FastAPI path].\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aname = \22Sarah\22\0Aday_of_week = \22Tuesday\22\0Aprint(\22Hello {name}! It is {day_of_week} today!\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aname = \22Sarah\22\0Aday_of_week = \22Tuesday\22\0Aprint(f\22Hello {name}! It is {day_of_week} today!\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis fix will always change the behavior of the program and, despite the precautions detailed\0Aabove, this may be undesired. As such the fix is always marked as unsafe.\0A\0A[logging]: https://docs.python.org/3/howto/logging-cookbook.html#using-particular-formatting-styles-throughout-your-application\0A[gettext]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/gettext.html\0A[FastAPI path]: https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/path-params/\0A", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1218 = private unnamed_addr constant [39 x i8] c"Possible f-string without an `f` prefix", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1219 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1218, [8 x i8] c"'\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1220 = private unnamed_addr constant [25 x i8] c"mutable-dataclass-default", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1221 = private unnamed_addr constant [1042 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for mutable default values in dataclass attributes.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AMutable default values share state across all instances of the dataclass.\0AThis can lead to bugs when the attributes are changed in one instance, as\0Athose changes will unexpectedly affect all other instances.\0A\0AInstead of sharing mutable defaults, use the `field(default_factory=...)`\0Apattern.\0A\0AIf the default value is intended to be mutable, it must be annotated with\0A`typing.ClassVar`; otherwise, a `ValueError` will be raised.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom dataclasses import dataclass\0A\0A\0A@dataclass\0Aclass A:\0A    # A list without a `default_factory` or `ClassVar` annotation\0A    # will raise a `ValueError`.\0A    mutable_default: list[int] = []\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\0A\0A\0A@dataclass\0Aclass A:\0A    mutable_default: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\0A```\0A\0AOr:\0A```python\0Afrom dataclasses import dataclass\0Afrom typing import ClassVar\0A\0A\0A@dataclass\0Aclass A:\0A    mutable_default: ClassVar[list[int]] = []\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1222 = private unnamed_addr constant [58 x i8] c"Do not use mutable default values for dataclass attributes", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1223 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1222, [8 x i8] c":\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1224 = private unnamed_addr constant [22 x i8] c"mutable-fromkeys-value", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1225 = private unnamed_addr constant [1413 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for mutable objects passed as a value argument to `dict.fromkeys`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAll values in the dictionary created by the `dict.fromkeys` method\0Arefer to the same instance of the provided object. If that object is\0Amodified, all values are modified, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\0AFor example, if the empty list (`[]`) is provided as the default value,\0Aall values in the dictionary will use the same list; as such, appending to\0Aany one entry will append to all entries.\0A\0AInstead, use a comprehension to generate a dictionary with distinct\0Ainstances of the default value.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Acities = dict.fromkeys([\22UK\22, \22Poland\22], [])\0Acities[\22UK\22].append(\22London\22)\0Acities[\22Poland\22].append(\22Poznan\22)\0Aprint(cities)  # {'UK': ['London', 'Poznan'], 'Poland': ['London', 'Poznan']}\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Acities = {country: [] for country in [\22UK\22, \22Poland\22]}\0Acities[\22UK\22].append(\22London\22)\0Acities[\22Poland\22].append(\22Poznan\22)\0Aprint(cities)  # {'UK': ['London'], 'Poland': ['Poznan']}\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as the edit will change the behavior of\0Athe program by using a distinct object for every value in the dictionary,\0Arather than a shared mutable instance. In some cases, programs may rely on\0Athe previous behavior.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `dict.fromkeys`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#dict.fromkeys)\0A", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1226 = private unnamed_addr constant [56 x i8] c"Do not pass mutable objects as values to `dict.fromkeys`", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1227 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1226, [8 x i8] c"8\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
