@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1236 = private unnamed_addr constant [26 x i8] c"error-instead-of-exception", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1237 = private unnamed_addr constant [1099 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `logging.error` instead of `logging.exception` when\0Alogging an exception.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`logging.exception` logs the exception and the traceback, while\0A`logging.error` only logs the exception. The former is more appropriate\0Awhen logging an exception, as the traceback is often useful for debugging.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0A\0Adef func():\0A    try:\0A        raise NotImplementedError\0A    except NotImplementedError:\0A        logging.error(\22Exception occurred\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0A\0Adef func():\0A    try:\0A        raise NotImplementedError\0A    except NotImplementedError:\0A        logging.exception(\22Exception occurred\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as safe when run against `logging.error` calls,\0Abut unsafe when marked against other logger-like calls (e.g.,\0A`logger.error`), since the rule is prone to false positives when detecting\0Alogger-like calls outside of the `logging` module.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `logging.exception`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html#logging.exception)\0A", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1238 = private unnamed_addr constant [50 x i8] c"Use `logging.exception` instead of `logging.error`", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1239 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1238, [8 x i8] c"2\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1240 = private unnamed_addr constant [17 x i8] c"try-consider-else", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1241 = private unnamed_addr constant [974 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `return` statements in `try` blocks.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `try`-`except` statement has an `else` clause for code that should\0Arun _only_ if no exceptions were raised. Returns in `try` blocks may\0Aexhibit confusing or unwanted behavior, such as being overridden by\0Acontrol flow in `except` and `finally` blocks, or unintentionally\0Asuppressing an exception.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0A\0Adef reciprocal(n):\0A    try:\0A        rec = 1 / n\0A        print(f\22reciprocal of {n} is {rec}\22)\0A        return rec\0A    except ZeroDivisionError:\0A        logging.exception(\22Exception occurred\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0A\0Adef reciprocal(n):\0A    try:\0A        rec = 1 / n\0A    except ZeroDivisionError:\0A        logging.exception(\22Exception occurred\22)\0A    else:\0A        print(f\22reciprocal of {n} is {rec}\22)\0A        return rec\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Errors and Exceptions](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/errors.html)\0A", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1242 = private unnamed_addr constant [49 x i8] c"Consider moving this statement to an `else` block", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1243 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1242, [8 x i8] c"1\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1244 = private unnamed_addr constant [19 x i8] c"verbose-log-message", align 1
@anon.a6b6a9a55df1a6fb8afb47e1f078e4b4.1245 = private unnamed_addr constant [488 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for excessive logging of exception objects.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen logging exceptions via `logging.exception`, the exception object\0Ais logged automatically. Including the exception object in the log\0Amessage is redundant and can lead to excessive logging.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    ...\0Aexcept ValueError as e:\0A    logger.exception(f\22Found an error: {e}\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    ...\0Aexcept ValueError:\0A    logger.exception(\22Found an error\22)\0A```\0A", align 1
